Successful Humanitarian Intervention With UNSC Authorisation - East Timor (1999-2000)
East Timor’s struggle for independence from Indonesia in 1999 was marked by severe violence
and human rights abuses following a UN-sponsored independence referendum. Pro-Indonesian
militias, with support from elements of the Indonesian military, conducted a brutal campaign of
retribution, displacing hundreds of thousands and causing widespread destruction.
The international community, led by Australia and backed by the UN, intervened through the
International Force for East Timor (INTERFET). The mission successfully restored order,
facilitated humanitarian aid, and enabled the transition to peace. The intervention was widely
considered successful due to strong regional support, effective leadership, and clear UN
authorisation. It demonstrated the potential effectiveness of rapid, well-coordinated interventions
in preventing further violence and stabilising a crisis.
Successful Humanitarian Intervention With UNSC Authorisation - Sierra Leone (1999-2005)
The civil war in Sierra Leone saw widespread atrocities, including the use of child soldiers and
severe human rights violations by the Revolutionary United Front (RUF). In 1999, the UN
authorised the deployment of UNAMSIL (United Nations Mission in Sierra Leone) to oversee the
peace process and disarm combatants. However, initial efforts struggled due to the limited
mandate and resistance from the RUF.
The intervention gained momentum when British forces intervened in 2000 to stabilise the
situation and protect the capital, Freetown. The British operation, combined with strengthened
UN efforts, effectively defeated the RUF and restored government control. The intervention in
Sierra Leone is considered successful due to its combination of international and national efforts
that led to disarmament, peace, and the establishment of the Special Court for Sierra Leone to
prosecute those responsible for war crimes.
Humanitarian Interventions In The 21st Century
Humanitarian Intervention From 2000-2011
The Responsibility To Protect (R2P)
What Is The Reponsibility To Protect Doctrine (R2P)?
● The Responsibility to Protect (R2P) was developed as a response to the humanitarian
failures of the 1990s, most notably the Rwandan Genocide and the Srebrenica massacre in
Bosnia, where international inaction allowed mass atrocities to occur.
● R2P set out that a nation state had a responsibility to protect the human rights of their own
citizens and that if they didn’t do so, other states had a responsibility to override their
sovereignty and intervene to prevent human rights abuses.
○ It emphasised that military intervention should only be pursued as a last resort, after
all peaceful measures had failed and when there was authorisation by the UN
Security Council.
● R2P was formally endorsed by the United Nations in 2005 during the World Summit, where
global leaders agreed on the principle that states have a responsibility to protect their
populations from genocide, war crimes, ethnic cleansing, and crimes against humanity.
○ The agreement was codified in the World Summit Outcome Document and later
referenced in UN General Assembly and Security Council resolutions.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 6
The Significance Of R2P Despite It Not Being Used:
● R2P marked a critical shift in international law by eroding the traditional concept of absolute
state sovereignty. It established that sovereignty was conditional on a state’s ability and
willingness to protect its population from severe human rights abuses.
○ If a state either failed in this responsibility or was itself the perpetrator, it could forfeit
its immunity from external interference.
○ This principle transformed the discourse on international intervention, framing it as a
moral and legal obligation under certain conditions.
● However, despite its theoretical significance, R2P was rarely explicitly invoked in major
interventions during the 2000s and early 2010s, such as in Iraq or Libya, where
humanitarian arguments were made but without formal reference to R2P.
The War On Terror And Its Consequences
The War on Terror, initiated by the United States following the 9/11 terrorist attacks in 2001,
marked a significant shift in U.S. foreign policy. The primary focus of this campaign was to combat
terrorism and prevent future attacks, leading to large-scale military operations in Afghanistan and
Iraq. Unlike previous humanitarian interventions that primarily aimed to protect human rights and
prevent mass atrocities, the War on Terror was driven by national security concerns. The
interventions were framed as preemptive measures to dismantle terrorist networks, such as
Al-Qaeda, and prevent the proliferation of weapons of mass destruction.
Lack of UN Authorisation and Its Implications:
● The invasions that characterised the War on Terror, particularly the invasion of Iraq in 2003,
were not authorised by the United Nations Security Council (UNSC). This was not only due
to anticipated vetoes from China and Russia, but also because the U.S. intervention had
little basis in international law.
○ This lack of UN endorsement was significant, as it marked a departure from the
post-Cold War norm of seeking multilateral consensus for military interventions.
● This not only undermined the legitimacy of the invasion of Iraq in particular, it also damaged
the US’ reputation as a superpower that supported the liberal international order and upheld
international law.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 7
The Use of Humanitarian Justifications:
● Despite the strategic and security-driven motives of the War on Terror, the U.S. government
frequently used humanitarian language to justify its military interventions.
○ The narrative included rhetoric about liberating oppressed populations, promoting
democracy, and protecting human rights.
● In the case of Iraq, President George W. Bush framed the war as a means to free the Iraqi
people from a brutal dictatorship that committed severe human rights violations, including
the use of chemical weapons against its own citizens and the persecution of minorities like
the Kurds and Shiites.
○ This co-opting of humanitarian rhetoric was aimed at bolstering domestic and
international support, even though the primary motives were strategic and
security-oriented.
Damage to the Reputation of the U.S. and Humanitarian Interventions
● The War on Terror, particularly the prolonged conflicts in Iraq and Afghanistan, significantly
damaged the reputation of the U.S. and the broader concept of humanitarian intervention.
○ Reports of human rights abuses by U.S. forces, including the use of torture at
Guantanamo Bay and Abu Ghraib prison, eroded the credibility of the U.S. as a
defender of human rights, as did the fact that an estimated 200,000 Iraqi civilians
were killed during the Iraq War.
○ The absence of discovered weapons of mass destruction in Iraq further undermined
the justification for the invasion and reinforced the perception that humanitarian
arguments were used as a cover for strategic objectives.
● The unilateral nature of these interventions and their devastating consequences tainted the
image of humanitarian intervention as a positive means to protect human rights. It led to
increased scepticism about the motivations behind military interventions and raised
concerns about the selective application of humanitarian principles to justify the West’s
strategic objectives.
