Humanitarian Intervention In Past 15 Years
Three Key Reasons for the Decline in Humanitarian Interventions
1. The Growth in Power of Countries That Oppose Any Violations of State Sovereignty:
● Over the past 15 years, the rise in power of China and Russia has significantly impacted
the global willingness and capacity to engage in humanitarian interventions.
○ Both countries have been strong supporters of the principle of state sovereignty and
have opposed any international actions that could be perceived as violating this
norm, even to protect human rights under the R2P doctrine.
● China and Russia have not only opposed interventions on principle but have shown a
willingness to use their UNSC vetoes to block resolutions that could authorise humanitarian
action, especially when such interventions might challenge their own strategic interests or
those of their allies.
○ For example, Russia has repeatedly vetoed resolutions aimed at addressing the
crisis in Syria, shielding its ally Bashar al-Assad from international military
responses despite evidence of widespread human rights abuses, including the use
of chemical weapons.
○ This assertive use of veto power has created an environment where major
humanitarian interventions are increasingly difficult, if not impossible, to pursue.
2. Growing Criticism of Western Hypocrisy/Selective Interventionism:
● The reputation of humanitarian intervention, particularly led by Western powers, has been
tarnished by accusations of hypocrisy and selective application.
○ The War on Terror, particularly the Iraq War and the use of torture at facilities like
Guantanamo Bay and Abu Ghraib, permanently damaged the credibility of the
United States and its allies.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 9
○ These events exposed a double standard where the West was perceived as
violating the very human rights it claimed to defend, eroding trust in its motivations
for future interventions.
● Beyond the War on Terror, criticism has grown over the West’s failure to hold its allies
accountable for human rights abuses.
○ Recently the reputation of the U.S. in particular has been damaged by the
Israel-Gaza war, where it has continued to arm and support Israel despite reports of
significant human rights abuses and over 45,000 deaths, the majority of whom have
been civilians and many of whom were children.
● This perceived hypocrisy weakened the moral authority of Western nations to lead or
support humanitarian interventions.
3. The United States’ Growing Isolationism:
● The decline in humanitarian interventions has also been influenced by the United States’
rejection of its previous role as the global police force and the shift instead towards
isolationism, which began in the late 2000s and has continued into the 2010s and 2020s.
● The election of President Barack Obama in 2008 reflected growing public disillusionment
with extensive military engagements abroad, particularly in light of the prolonged and costly
wars in Iraq and Afghanistan.
○ Obama, who had opposed the Iraq War, was elected on a programme of decreasing
the involvement of U.S. troops abroad, even though he didn’t make major progress
on this as President, with the war in Afghanistan continuing.
● This trend deepened under President Donald Trump, who advocated for an “America First”
approach that prioritised domestic issues over international interventions.
○ Trump’s administration marked a significant departure from the U.S.’s historical role
as the global enforcer of liberal democratic values and human rights, further eroding
the impetus for humanitarian missions.
○ Under President Joe Biden, the withdrawal from Afghanistan in 2021 reinforced this
isolationist stance.
● The shift towards a more inward-focused U.S. foreign policy has left a vacuum in global
leadership, contributing to the overall decline in humanitarian actions in the past 15 years.
Human Rights Violations That Have Been Allowed To Happen
Over the past 15 years, numerous human rights violations have occurred without international
humanitarian intervention. While some of these would not have attracted intervention in the 1990s
due to occurring in powerful states or those backed by major powers like Russia and China, others
in weaker nations might have previously led to interventions. Below are examples of major human
rights violations that have taken place without humanitarian intervention:
Uyghur Genocide in China: Reports from the past decade detail mass detentions of Uyghur
Muslims in Xinjiang by the Chinese government. Detention camps, forced labour, forced
assimilation policies, and allegations of sterilisation have been documented, amounting to what
many have called a genocide. Despite global condemnation and sanctions from some countries,
significant intervention has not been feasible due to China’s power and influence.
Rohingya Genocide: In Myanmar, the Rohingya Muslim minority has faced severe persecution,
including mass killings, sexual violence, and forced displacement. The military crackdown in 2017
led to hundreds of thousands fleeing to Bangladesh. While the ICJ has taken up the case,
substantial intervention to protect the Rohingya has been absent.
