mass killings, sexual violence, and ethnically targeted attacks have been documented, further
escalating the crisis. While the international community has expressed concern and provided
humanitarian aid, there has been no substantial military intervention to protect civilians.
The Debate About Humanitarian Interventions
Arguments That Humanitarian Interventions Do Promote Human Rights
Humanitarian Interventions Are Crucial To Preventing The Immediate Human Rights
Violations of Civilians
● One of the most compelling arguments for humanitarian interventions is their role in
preventing immediate and severe human rights violations against civilians.
● When mass atrocities such as genocide, ethnic cleansing, or systematic violence are
imminent or underway, military intervention can be the only effective means to stop or
mitigate the suffering.
○ For example, the NATO intervention in Kosovo (1999) was launched to prevent
large-scale ethnic violence and protect the Albanian population from massacres by
Serbian forces. This action was critical in halting violence and safeguarding the lives
of civilians who faced existential threats.
● Similarly, the INTERFET mission in East Timor (1999) effectively ended widespread
violence following the independence referendum by deploying forces that restored order
and protected civilians from pro-Indonesian militias.
● These examples demonstrate how timely interventions are crucial to preserving lives and
preventing further atrocities when peaceful or diplomatic options have failed.
Humanitarian Interventions Uphold Human Rights Through The R2P and Are An
Important Deterrence
● The doctrine of Responsibility to Protect (R2P), formally endorsed by the United Nations in
2005, represents a commitment by the international community to uphold human rights and
act when states fail to protect their populations from mass atrocities.
● Humanitarian interventions under R2P serve not only as a response mechanism but also as
a deterrent. The existence of this principle signals to potential perpetrators that the global
community is prepared to intervene if large-scale human rights violations occur.
○ This preventive aspect of humanitarian intervention reinforces global human rights
norms by underscoring the principle that state sovereignty is conditional upon the
protection of citizens.
● The NATO intervention in Libya (2011), although controversial, demonstrated this aspect by
acting under the banner of protecting civilians when Muammar Gaddafi’s forces threatened
mass killings in Benghazi.
○ While the aftermath raised questions about post-conflict planning, the intervention
itself showcased how invoking R2P can lead to decisive action aimed at preventing
