mass human rights violations and reinforcing the idea that the international
community holds a shared responsibility to protect vulnerable populations.
Some Humanitarian Interventions Have Been Successful In Lasting Peace and
Holding Perpetrators Accountable
● There have been instances where humanitarian interventions have not only halted
immediate human rights abuses but also contributed to lasting peace and accountability for
perpetrators.
● The intervention in Sierra Leone (1999-2005) exemplifies a successful case where external
military action supported broader peace efforts and upheld human rights in the long term.
○ The deployment of UNAMSIL and the later involvement of British forces helped to
stabilise the country, defeat the Revolutionary United Front (RUF), and restore
governmental control.
○ This intervention was crucial in disarming combatants and laying the groundwork for
peace. Additionally, the establishment of the Special Court for Sierra Leone ensured
that those responsible for war crimes were prosecuted, including Charles Taylor,
who was later convicted.
○ These actions not only ended immediate violence but also reinforced international
legal norms by holding leaders accountable for human rights abuses.
● This case illustrates how well-executed humanitarian interventions can promote long-term
stability, uphold justice, and demonstrate that violations of human rights will be met with
consequences.
Arguments That Humanitarian Interventions Don’t Promote Human Rights
Humanitarian Interventions Undermine State Sovereignty and Risk Any Military
Intervention Being Justified on Humanitarian Grounds
● Humanitarian interventions have set a precedent that undermines the principle of state
sovereignty, raising concerns about how these interventions might be used as justification
for military actions that are not genuinely aimed at protecting human rights.
○ The concept of Responsibility to Protect (R2P), while noble in intent, has contributed
to this risk by codifying the idea that states lose certain sovereign protections when
they fail to safeguard their citizens.
● Critics argue that this has blurred the lines between genuine humanitarian intervention and
strategic military action, making it easier for powerful nations to invoke humanitarian
rhetoric as a cover for pursuing their own political or economic interests.
○ For instance, the Iraq War (2003), though primarily justified by the U.S. on the
grounds of eliminating weapons of mass destruction, also included humanitarian
justifications to remove Saddam Hussein and protect Iraqis from his regime’s
abuses.
○ However, this action lacked UN authorisation and was later widely seen as a misuse
of humanitarian rhetoric to legitimise a strategic operation.
Humanitarian Interventions Have Caused Major Civilian Casualties and Are
Selectively Used by the West
● One of the strongest criticisms of humanitarian interventions is that they are selectively
used by Western powers and often lead to significant civilian casualties.
● Military interventions, even those justified by humanitarian motives, inherently carry the risk
of collateral damage that can undermine the very human rights they aim to protect.
