● In 2024, civil society mobilised once again in response to Israel’s military actions in Gaza.
Protests erupted globally, with millions demonstrating in cities across Europe, North
America, and the Middle East to denounce the high civilian death toll and call for immediate
humanitarian aid access.
○ Social media played a critical role in spreading information and coordinating
demonstrations, with the #CeasefireNow hashtag trending globally.
○ These demonstrations have intensified public debate on the responsibility of
governments to respond to alleged human rights abuses and have led to growing
calls for accountability, with several governments facing pressure to reassess
military aid policies and diplomatic relations with Israel.
○ This surge in activism exemplifies how grassroots movements continue to amplify
human rights issues, placing them at the forefront of public consciousness and
international discourse.
Human Rights NGOs
● Human rights organisations like Amnesty International, Human Rights Watch (HRW), and
the International Federation for Human Rights (FIDH) play a critical role in global human
rights advocacy by documenting abuses, campaigning for policy changes, and providing
legal support.
● Amnesty International, with over 10 million members and supporters globally, has leveraged
its extensive network to spotlight issues like political imprisonment, torture, and unlawful
killings.
○ Amnesty’s ongoing reports on forced labor in Xinjiang have drawn international
attention and led to sanctions from the European Union and United States against
Chinese officials.
● Human Rights Watch similarly has a significant influence, producing in-depth reports on
issues such as child labor in agriculture, human trafficking, and state-sponsored violence.
○ HRW’s work on Rohingya abuses in Myanmar played a key role in the International
Court of Justice (ICJ) taking up the genocide case against Myanmar.
● In 2021, FIDH’s lobbying efforts contributed to the EU’s adoption of the Global Human
Rights Sanctions Regime, allowing the EU to impose sanctions on human rights violators
worldwide. This marked a significant step in holding state and non-state actors
accountable, enhancing the capacity of NGOs to drive international policy.
Foundations and Philanthropists
● Major foundations and philanthropists have also sought to promote human rights, funding
initiatives that address systemic injustices and empowering grassroots organisations.
